Use the identity \[(a+b)(a-b) = a^2-b^2\] to multiply the expressions by the conjugates. Then we have:

\[\begin{array}{rl}
&\begin{aligned}
\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}+1}&=\dfrac{1(\sqrt{2}-1)}{(\sqrt{2}+1)(\sqrt{2}-1)}=
\dfrac{\sqrt{2}-1}{2-1}
&
&\begin{aligned}
\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{3}-1}&=\dfrac{2(\sqrt{3}+1)}{(\sqrt{3}-1)(\sqrt{3}+1)}=
\dfrac{2(\sqrt{3}+1)}{3-1}
\end{aligned}
\end{aligned}
\\
\end{array}\]

We have successfully rationalized the expressions.

Next, simplify the expression \[\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}+1}+\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{3}-1}\] by adding and cross multiplying the numerators and denominators:

\[\begin{aligned}
&\left(\sqrt{2}-1\right)+\left(2\sqrt{3}+2\right)
&
&\left(
\sqrt{2}\left\{
\sqrt{3}+1+\sqrt{6}+\sqrt{2}\right\}-\left\{
1+\sqrt{2}+2\right\}
\right)\left\{
\sqrt{2}\left\{
\sqrt{3}+1+\sqrt{6}-\sqrt{2}\right\}-\left\{
1-\sqrt{2}+2\right\}
\right\}
\end{aligned}\]

After further simplification, we arrive at the simplified expression \[\boxed{\dfrac{5-2\sqrt{2}+2\sqrt{3}+\sqrt{6}}{5-2\sqrt{2}}}\]

The next step is to rationalize the denominator. Multiply both the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator, which is $5+2\sqrt{2}$.

\[\begin{aligned}
&\dfrac{(5-2\sqrt{2}+2\sqrt{3}+\sqrt{6})(5+2\sqrt{2})}{(5-2\sqrt{2})(5+2\sqrt{2})}
\end{aligned}\]

After multiplying out the numerator and simplifying the expression, we arrive at the final answer:

\[\boxed{\dfrac{39+10\sqrt{6}}{17}}\]

