label("$\parbox{1cm}{There are \\ 10 numbers \\ less than or equal to 8}$",(-10,2));
label("$\parbox{1cm}{There are \\ 15 numbers \\ less than or equal to 9}$",(-10,0.5));
yaxis(-1.5,11.5);
[/asy] Thus, since each number is equally likely, we can divide our solution space into the above cases, finding the probability of landing on a number in the respective ranges in each case below.  If Jeff starts at 1, he has $\frac{6}{10}$ chance of ending up in the range {$1 \dots 6$}, $\frac{4}{10}$ chance of ending up in the range {$7 \dots 10$} and a $\frac{0}{10}$ chance of ending up in the range {$-10 \dots -4$} since $\frac{6}{10}+\frac{4}{10}+\frac{0}{10}=1$.  For 2, he has $\frac{10}{10}$ chance of ending up in the range {$2 \dots 11$}, $\frac{0}{10}$ chance of ending up in the range {$-10 \dots 1$} since $\frac{10}{10}+\frac{0}{10}=1$.  Continuing, we find that the probability to end up in the desired region is $\frac{6}{10}\cdot\frac{6}{10}+\frac{4}{10}\cdot \frac{4}{10}+\frac{4}{10}\cdot\frac{10}{10}=0.64$ which gives an answer of $\boxed{\frac{16}{25}}$.[/asy]
